    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d517">

                <pb facs="0628" n="622" sameAs="frus:frus1942v03/pg622"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/23495: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Standley</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-08-14T12:00:00+03:00">August 14,
                            1942—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received August 14—9:39 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>296. Mr. Harriman is reporting his conversations here direct to the President
                    through Navy codes and is keeping me fully informed.<anchor n="22"
                        xml:id="d517-f1"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Standley</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d517-f1" n="22" xml:id="d517-f1-content">
                <p>No telegrams of significance from Harriman relating to the Moscow conversations
                    with Stalin, in addition to those here printed, have been found in Department
                    files or at the Franklin D. Roosevelt Library, Hyde Park, N. Y.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
